# Usage

The examples below describe the basic usage of the Checkstyle Plugin.

Generate Checkstyle Report As Part of the Project Reports
---------------------------------------------------------

Add the Checkstyle Plugin to the `<reporting>` section of your `pom.xml`:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting>
  ...
</project>
```

Run the `site` phase to generate the report in `target/site/checkstyle.html`:

```shell
mvn site
```

In a multi-module build, you can add one report for all modules with the `checkstyle-aggregate` report.
List the reports you want in a `<reportSet>`:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <reportSets>
          <reportSet>
            <reports>
              <report>checkstyle</report>
              <report>checkstyle-aggregate</report>
            </reports>
          </reportSet>
        </reportSets>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting>
  ...
</project>
```

Generate Checkstyle Report As Standalone
----------------------------------------

You can also generate the Checkstyle report from the command line.
You do not need to declare the plugin in your `pom.xml` unless you need a specific configuration.

```shell
mvn checkstyle:checkstyle
```

The report is written to `target/reports/checkstyle.html`.
For a multi-module build, run `mvn checkstyle:checkstyle-aggregate` from the root project; it writes `target/reports/checkstyle-aggregate.html`.

<a id="Checking_for_Violations_as_Part_of_the_Build"></a>

Check for Violations as Part of the Build
-----------------------------------------

To log violations to the console and fail the build, add an execution of the `check` goal to the `<build>` section.
Options that you set in the `<reporting>` section do not affect executions in the `<build>` section.

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <configLocation>checkstyle.xml</configLocation>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>validate</id>
            <phase>validate</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>check</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  ...
</project>
```

The phase that you bind `check` to matters:

- Bound to `validate`, as above, it runs before compilation, so it also runs on `mvn compile` and `mvn test`.
  For code that does not compile, Checkstyle can report different errors than the `javac` compiler.
- Without a `<phase>`, it binds to `verify`.
  The compiler then flags invalid code first, but `mvn test` does not run the check.

By default, only violations of severity `error` are logged and fail the build.
Set `violationSeverity` to `warning` to include warnings, or `failOnViolation` to `false` to log violations without failing.
To skip the check for one build, pass `-Dcheckstyle.skip`.
